#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# Zeitlos ask -- the host-side distribution builder.
#
#   ./tools/ask/ask sources                     list source adapters
#   ./tools/ask/ask ingest  dist/minimal.spec   read sources, report
#   ./tools/ask/ask build   dist/minimal.spec   full build into out/
#   ./tools/ask/ask eval    dist/minimal.spec   recall against the gold set
#   ./tools/ask/ask query   dist/minimal.spec "how do I purify water"
#
# -- why build and train and release are three separate things --
#
# A distribution is not part of a Zeitlos release and must not be tied
# to one. Releases go out monthly; a corpus is rebuilt when the corpus
# changes, and a model is retrained when there is a reason to, which
# may be once a year. So this tool produces a self-contained directory
# tree that is unzipped onto a card, carries its own version, and is
# checked at load time against nothing but its own dataset id.
#
# release/ picks up a built distribution IF ONE IS PRESENT and ships
# without it otherwise. See docs/ask_app.md, "Shipping".
#
# -- reproducibility --
#
# Same recipe plus same inputs produces the same bytes, so two builds
# can be diffed and a distribution can be rebuilt from its manifest
# years later. Everything that could introduce nondeterminism is
# pinned: source order is sorted, card numbering is sequential in
# sorted order, and no timestamp goes anywhere except the manifest's
# own `built` field (which is excluded from the build hash).
#

